---
import Layout from '../../layouts/Layout.astro';
import Button from '../../components/Button.astro';

// Images
import dpmock from '/images/design-packages-mockup.jpg';

import optionflow from '/images/option-flow.jpg';
import workshops from '/images/workshops.jpg';
import search from '/images/search.jpg';
import selectproduct from '/images/select-product.jpg';
import autocreation from '/images/auto-creation.jpg';
import datavisuals from '/images/data-visuals.jpg';
---

<Layout title="Case Study: Design Packages">
	<main>
		<h1>Case Study: <span class="text-gradient">Design Packages</span></h1>

		<img src={dpmock} width="100%" alt="Design Packages Product Slots" class="margin-top-0" />
		<p class="img-alt">Partially-filled Products Slots in Electrical Fixtures.</p>

		<div class="button-container">
			<Button
				href="https://www.figma.com/proto/TEvabCODkxgB8FZuyA1w0Q/Q2-2023-_-%F0%9F%8F%A0-Design-Packages?page-id=7906%3A93958&node-id=11-52084&viewport=-603%2C101%2C0.17&t=kqdDfua8G7SUXEWe-1&scaling=scale-down&content-scaling=fixed&starting-point-node-id=11%3A52084"
				target="_blank"
				text="View Select Product demo"
			/>
		</div>

		<b class="hr"></b>

		<section>
			<div class="left-column">
				<div data-lightbox="true">
					<img src={autocreation} width="100%" alt="Design Packages auto-creating a new Product Card with unallocated options." />
				</div>
				<p class="img-alt">Design Packages auto-creating a new Product Card with unallocated options.</p>
			</div>
			<div class="right-column">
				<h2 class="margin-top-0">Development-Level Interior Design</h2>
				<p>Design Packages is an app for Interior Designers to select finish products for homes in large residential developments, allowing for buyer customization. It also supports Architects in defining exterior materials, Procurement Agents in making purchasing decisions, and Program Managers in setting budgets and product constraints. As part of the Blueprint rebuild, the app must support Homebound's internal operations and be ready for future white-labeling. </p>
				
				<h3>Construction Disruptor</h3>
				<p>Homebound is a residential home builder aiming to disrupt the construction industry with a comprehensive software solution for data collection, takeoffs, design packages, plan management, buyer checkout creation, data analysis, and everything in-between.</p>
			</div>
		</section>

		<section>
			<div class="left-column">
				<div data-lightbox="true" class="start-margin-3">
					<img src={optionflow} width="100%" alt="How optioned products flow through the steps of our system." />
				</div>
				<p class="img-alt">How optioned products flow through the steps of our system.</p>
			</div>
			<div class="right-column">
				<h3>My Role</h3>
				<p>As the sole designer on one of four squads rebuilding Blueprint, I led the discovery, UX, and product design of Design Packages. Our team also developed the Materials Catalog and Option Management System. I collaborated daily with the squad's Product Manager, Lead Developer, and a team of full-stack developers. The project demanded intricate system thinking and close teamwork. I designed everything in Figma using our in-house design system, and our app was built in React.</p>
			</div>
		</section>

		<section>
			<div class="left-column">
				<div data-lightbox="true" class="start-margin-3">
					<img src={search} width="100%" alt="Product Search for a semi-flushmount light fixtures in Design Packages." />
				</div>
				<p class="img-alt">Product Search for a semi-flushmount light fixtures in Design Packages.</p>
			</div>
			<div class="right-column">
				<h3>Goals and Challenges</h3>
				<p>Our primary goal was to simplify and streamline the creation of Design Packages, ensuring reusability and comprehensive data collection to increase efficiency and reduce margins. The Interior Design team previously used Google Slides for buyer-facing Design Packages and spreadsheets for construction Finish Schedules. We aimed to minimize redundant work and enhance data collection. Additionally, we wanted to provide program managers with tools to create and enforce budgets, and equip Procurement Agents with product data for bids and communication with Interior Designers about budget constraints and product availability.</p>
				<p>Our biggest challenge was the tight timeline. Our small tech team had just 4 months to prototype a complex platform, with the MVP to be used by internal teams for a new development in Houston. The rebuild was divided into four main apps: Plan Creation, Design Packages, Product Offering, and Build Task Management. Each squad had one designer, and we met regularly to ensure cross-squad decisions and design consistency. The option management system was particularly complex, as it had to allow designers to assign options for buyer customization across an entire development and work for architects creating plans and takeoffs, with data flowing through the system to generate the buyer checkout. The biggest challenge was often not just the complexity, but considering all possible edge cases and refining ideas to create a streamlined MVP.</p>
			</div>
		</section>

		<section>
			<div class="left-column">
				<div data-lightbox="true" class="start-margin-3">
					<img src={workshops} width="100%" class="margin-top-16" alt="Card sorting and mind map during early discover stages." />
				</div>
				<p class="img-alt">Card sorting and mind map during early discover stages.</p>
			</div>
			<div class="right-column">
				<h3>Our Process</h3>
				<p>We conducted frequent rounds of interviews with our Interior Design, Procurement, Architecture, and Program Management teams. The Product Design team traveled across the country to meet team members for in-depth discussions about their work challenges. I led several workshops to delve deeper into these issues and identify opportunities for Design Packages. We used data from card sorting, Kano models, Red Routes, and surveys from these workshops to create initial feature prioritization suggestions.</p>
				<p>The designs underwent multiple iterations, with regular large-scale demos presented to the leadership team. Feedback and user testing guided our design updates and altered our approach to micro-systems several times. Due to time constraints, we used WSJF to prioritize feature development within a Kanban process.</p>
			</div>
		</section>

		<section>
			<div class="left-column">
				<div data-lightbox="true" class="start-margin-3">
					<img src={selectproduct} width="100%" alt="Product Detail page where you can select Package Options for a product." />
				</div>
				<p class="img-alt">Product Detail page where you can select Package Options for a product.</p>
			</div>
			<div class="right-column">
				<h3>The Solution</h3>
				<p>Our company's buyer-customization options were organized into mutually exclusive "Package Options," such as Spec Level, Style, and Scheme. When creating a new Design Package, Interior Designers would select the options for the development and tag product selections accordingly. Product Cards displayed these selections with abbreviated tags for the assigned packages. This streamlined selection and assignment. If an item slot (e.g., Refrigerator) lacked a product for every option combination, Design Packages auto-created a new slot with unallocated options to ensure package completion. Item slots in our takeoffs had specific required attributes (e.g., 36" width for refrigerators), guiding designers and pre-filtering product search results. The Procurement Team's Finish Schedules were generated from Design Packages, providing a view with necessary data for purchasing and construction.</p>
			</div>
		</section>

		<section>
			<div class="left-column">
				<div data-lightbox="true" class="start-margin-3">
					<img src={datavisuals} width="100%" alt="Option completion data and Budget tracking." />
				</div>
				<p class="img-alt">Option completion data and Budget tracking.</p>
			</div>
			<div class="right-column">
				<h3>Reception and Future Considerations</h3>
				<p>Initially, finding an app team for the design system adoption was challenging, hindering real-life testing. However, we partnered with a team redesigning the owner account web app. As consultants, we actively participated in their scrum ceremonies, addressing component variations and adjustments. The teams synchronized efforts, enabling a smooth development process. We also introduced guest and owner variations to align with distinct app color schemes, ensuring differentiation.</p>
				<p>In the future, we aim to involve our Marketing team as contributors to our usage guidelines in Zeroheight. We plan to conduct user testing and A/B testing once the implementation progresses, aiming for success and buy-in from other teams. Expanding our design system and securing resources for future implementations will be driven by data and positive results.</p>
			</div>
		</section>

		<b class="hr"></b>

		<div class="button-container">
			<Button
				href="https://www.figma.com/proto/1BEIAWsKWgqbgWaeWyu5c1/EDS_-Components?page-id=6119%3A30505&type=design&node-id=8595-101204&viewport=830%2C536%2C0.15&t=JFo0nCQyfjzUZMWH-1&scaling=min-zoom&starting-point-node-id=8595%3A101204&mode=design"
				target="_blank"
				text="View component demo"
			/>
		</div>
		-->
	</main>
</Layout>

<style lang="scss">
	main {

		h1 {
			font-size: 2.25rem;
			font-weight: 500;
			margin-block-start: 0;
			margin-block-end: 2.5rem;
		}

		img {
			margin-top: .5rem;
		}
	}

	.button-container {
		display: flex;
		flex-direction: column;
		padding: 1rem 0 2rem;
	}

	h2 {
		font-size: 1.75rem;
		margin-block-start: 2rem;
		margin-block-end: 1rem;
	}
	h3 {
		margin-block-start: 2rem;
		font-size: 1.25rem;
	}
	p {
		margin-block-start: 1rem;
		margin-block-end: 1rem;
		font-size: 1.125rem;
	}

	@media only screen and (min-width: 768px) {

	main {

		h1 {
			font-size: 2.75rem;
		}
	}

	.button-container {
		align-items: center;
	}

	h2 {
		font-size: 2rem;
		margin-block-start: 3rem;
		margin-block-end: 1.25rem;
	}
	h3 {
		margin-block-start: 3rem;
		font-size: 1.5rem;
	}
	}
	
	@media only screen and (min-width: 1024px) {
		section {
			display: flex;
			flex-direction: row;

			.left-column {

				.img-alt:last-of-type {
					margin-block-end: 0;
				}
			}
		}
		.left-column {
			display: flex;
			flex-direction: column;
			flex: 1;
			padding-right: 2rem;
		}
		.right-column {
			width: 70ch;
		}
		.start-margin-3 {
			margin-top: 3rem;
		}
	}
</style>
